      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "When HIV is mentioned in a surgical forum a debate will often follow about \"universal testing\" and \"barrier precautions\". Unfortunately, there is little discussion of the many ways that HIV-infected patients may present primarily to the surgeon. Although lifestyle risk factors are important clues, unusual clinical features of a relatively common surgical disease should raise the suspicion of HIV infection."},
